# Public DNS-over-HTTPS resolvers, as a pf table.
#
# Port 53 is intercepted and port 853 is blocked, so a client that wants its
# own resolver has one way left: HTTPS on 443, which looks like any other web
# traffic. This closes the part of that which is closable -- the well-known
# public resolvers, reachable at stable anycast addresses.
#
# What it does NOT close, and cannot: DoH served from an address that also
# serves ordinary web content. Blocking those means blocking the sites sharing
# them. That is why only dedicated resolver addresses are listed here and no
# CDN ranges: cloudflare-dns.com also answers on 104.16.248.249, inside
# Cloudflare's shared CDN space, and blocking that /32 risks taking an
# unrelated site down with it.
#
# The router's own forwarders (1.1.1.1, 9.9.9.9 and friends on port 53) are
# unaffected: the rule using this table matches inbound on the LAN interface
# from the LAN network, and the resolver's own queries are neither.
#
# One address or network per line. Read by pf at ruleset load.

